<section xml:id="ws-mod1-bridge-reading">
    <title>Module 1: Bridge Reading Guide</title>
    <subtitle>Bauldry Chapter 2, pp. 45&#x2013;50 — Estimated time: 35 minutes</subtitle>

    <!-- ============================================================ -->

      <introduction>
        <p>
          This guide bridges Module 1 to the text you will be reading on
          day one of MAT 5610. The assignment is to read the opening six
          pages of Bauldry Chapter 2 (<q>Introduction to Real Analysis</q>),
          specifically pages 45&#x2013;50, which cover the chapter
          introduction and &#xa7;2.1 (Basic Topology of the Real
          Numbers) through the end of Theorem 2.7.
        </p>
        <p>
          <alert>Your goal is not to master the topology.</alert> That
          material will be developed carefully in Modules 2 and 3. Your
          goal here is narrower and more focused: to see how the logical
          language you practiced in Module 1 — quantifiers, negation,
          implication, proof strategies — appears immediately and everywhere
          in Bauldry's text. Every definition and theorem in these pages
          is a quantified statement. Every proof deploys one of the
          strategies you have just practiced.
        </p>
        <p>
          <alert>How to read.</alert> Read with a pencil. Before answering
          each question below, mark up the Bauldry page: box the quantifiers,
          underline hypotheses, circle conclusions. This active reading
          habit is essential in MAT 5610.
        </p>
        <p>
          <alert>A note on approach.</alert> If this material feels familiar
          from a prior real analysis course, use this reading to practice
          working at full speed with pencil in hand, focusing especially on
          the logical structure questions rather than the content. If this
          is your first encounter with analysis, read slowly and work every
          question in order. It is normal for some of the topology vocabulary
          (open, closed, accumulation point) to be unfamiliar — flag those
          terms and continue. The questions are designed so that you can
          answer them using the tools from Module 1 alone.
        </p>
        <p>
          <alert>Part 1: The Chapter Introduction (p. 45)</alert>
          (Suggested time: 5 minutes)
        </p>
        <p>
          Read the chapter introduction on page 45 (the paragraph beginning
          <q>We begin our study of real analysis&#x2026;</q>).
        </p>
      </introduction>

      <exercise xml:id="ex-mod1-br-1">
        <title>Logical Development vs. Calculation</title>
        <statement>
          <p>
            Bauldry describes Chapter 2 as covering <q>essentially the same
            topics as Chapter 1&#x2026; but now from a perspective based on
            logical development and proof.</q> Based on what you know from
            the Module 1 orientation, what does it mean for a mathematical
            development to be <em>based on logical development and proof</em>
            rather than just on calculation? What specific tools from
            Module 1 make this shift possible?
          </p>
        </statement>
      </exercise>

      <exercise xml:id="ex-mod1-br-2">
        <title>Think Deeply of Simple Things</title>
        <statement>
          <p> Bauldry quotes Arnold Ross: <q>think deeply of
            simple things.</q> How does this motto connect to the proof-writing
            advice in Zorn &#xa7;1.4 (your Study Guide Reading 1)?
            Identify one specific rule from Zorn that exemplifies this motto.
          </p>
        </statement>
      </exercise>

      <introduction>
        <p>
          <alert>Part 2: Reading Definitions as Quantified Statements
          (pp. 46&#x2013;49)</alert>
          (Suggested time: 15 minutes)
        </p>
        <p>
          The heart of this bridge reading is learning to parse Bauldry's
          definitions using the quantifier machinery from Module 1. Work
          through the questions below as you read the definitions on
          pp. 46&#x2013;49.
        </p>
        <p>
          <alert>Definition 2.1: Least Upper Bound Property (p. 47).</alert>
          Bauldry writes: <q><m>U</m> has the least upper bound property
          if, whenever a nonempty subset <m>E \subseteq U</m> is bounded
          above, then <m>E</m> has a least upper bound in <m>U</m>.</q>
          This is an implication embedded inside a universal claim.
        </p>
      </introduction>

      <exercise xml:id="ex-mod1-br-3">
        <title>LUB Property in Quantifier Notation</title>
        <statement>
          <p> Rewrite the Least Upper Bound Property in
            explicit quantifier notation. Use the following setup:
            let the universe be an ordered set <m>U</m>, let <m>E</m>
            range over nonempty subsets of <m>U</m>, and let
            <q><m>E</m> is bounded above</q> and
            <q><m>E</m> has a least upper bound in <m>U</m></q> be
            predicates. Your symbolic statement should have the form
            <m>\forall E \ldots \bigl( P(E) \Rightarrow Q(E) \bigr)</m>.
          </p>
        </statement>
      </exercise>

      <exercise xml:id="ex-mod1-br-4">
        <title>Negating the LUB Property</title>
        <statement>
          <p>
            Write the <em>negation</em> of the Least Upper Bound Property.
            In plain English, what would it mean for a set <m>U</m>
            to <em>fail</em> to have the least upper bound property?
            (Bauldry says <m>\Q</m> fails to have this property.
            Can you give a specific example?)
          </p>
        </statement>
      </exercise>


    <!-- ============================================================ -->

      <introduction>
        <p>
          <alert>Theorem 2.3: Archimedean Order Property (p. 48).</alert>
          Theorem 2.3 states: <em>If <m>\varepsilon</m> and <m>x</m> are
          positive real numbers, then there is an <m>n \in \N</m>
          such that <m>n\varepsilon \gt x</m>.</em>
          Compare Corollary 2.4, Item 3: <em>For any positive real number
          <m>x</m>, there is an <m>n \in \N</m> such that
          <m>1/n \lt x</m>.</em>
        </p>
      </introduction>

      <exercise xml:id="ex-mod1-br-5">
        <title>Archimedean Property in Quantifier Notation</title>
        <statement>
          <p> Write Theorem 2.3 in full quantifier notation,
            identifying: (a) the universal quantifiers and their domains;
            (b) the existential quantifier and its domain; (c) the
            predicate that must hold. Then compare your formula to the
            statement you translated in Worked Example 1 (Archimedean
            property). Are they the same statement?
          </p>
        </statement>
      </exercise>

      <exercise xml:id="ex-mod1-br-6">
        <title>Reading Bauldry's Proof of Theorem 2.3</title>
        <statement>
          <p> Read Bauldry's proof of Theorem 2.3 carefully.
            <ol marker="(a)">
              <li>
                What proof strategy does Bauldry use? (Direct,
                contrapositive, or contradiction?) How do you know?
              </li>
              <li>
                Identify the sentence where Bauldry assumes the
                opposite of the conclusion. Write it out.
              </li>
              <li>
                What is the contradiction Bauldry derives, and
                which theorem does he invoke to derive it?
              </li>
            </ol>
          </p>
        </statement>
      </exercise>

      <exercise xml:id="ex-mod1-br-7">
        <title>Corollary 2.4, Item 3: Negation and Structure</title>
        <statement>
          <p>
            Corollary 2.4 lists four equivalent forms of the Archimedean
            property. For Item 3 (<m>\forall x \gt 0 \; \exists n \in
            \N</m> s.t. <m>1/n \lt x</m>):
            <ol marker="(a)">
              <li>
                Write the negation in symbols and in plain English.
              </li>
              <li>
                Compare the negation to your answer in Practice Problem
                1-3(c) (negation of the <m>\varepsilon</m>-<m>\delta</m>
                statement). What structural similarity do you notice?
              </li>
            </ol>
          </p>
        </statement>
      </exercise>

      <introduction>
        <p>
          <alert>Definitions 2.2 and 2.3: Metric Spaces and Neighborhoods
          (pp. 48&#x2013;49).</alert>
          Bauldry defines a <term>metric space</term> as a set <m>X</m>
          with a distance function <m>d : X \to \R</m> satisfying
          three properties, and then defines a <term>neighborhood</term>
          of a point <m>x</m> as
          <m>N_r(x) = \{y \mid d(x, y) \lt r\}</m> for some <m>r \gt 0</m>.
        </p>
      </introduction>

      <exercise xml:id="ex-mod1-br-8">
        <title>Metric Properties in Quantifier Notation</title>
        <statement>
          <p>
            The three properties of a metric are:
            <ol marker="(a)">
              <li><m>d(x,y) = 0</m> if and only if <m>x = y</m></li>
              <li><m>d(x,y) = d(y,x)</m></li>
              <li><m>d(x,z) \leq d(x,y) + d(y,z)</m></li>
            </ol>
            Each of these is implicitly universally quantified over all
            <m>x, y, z \in X</m>. Write property 1 out in full quantifier
            notation, including the <q>if and only if</q> structure.
            (You will need two implications; recall how iff proofs work
            from Module 1.)
          </p>
        </statement>
      </exercise>

      <exercise xml:id="ex-mod1-br-9">
        <title>Accumulation Points</title>
        <statement>
          <p>
            Definition 2.3 defines a point <m>x</m> to be an
            <term>accumulation point</term> (or limit point) of a set
            <m>E</m> if every deleted neighborhood of <m>x</m> contains
            a point of <m>E</m> different from <m>x</m>; that is,
            <me>
              \forall r \gt 0, \; N_r'(x) \cap E \neq \emptyset.
            </me>
            <ol marker="(a)">
              <li>
                Write the negation of this statement. What would it mean
                for <m>x</m> to <em>not</em> be an accumulation point
                of <m>E</m>?
              </li>
              <li>
                Bauldry calls a point that is not an accumulation point
                an <term>isolated point</term>. Does your negation match
                Definition 2.3 item 8?
              </li>
            </ol>
          </p>
        </statement>
      </exercise>


    <!-- ============================================================ -->

      <introduction>
        <p>
          <alert>Part 3: Reading a Graduate-Level Proof (pp. 47&#x2013;50)</alert>
          (Suggested time: 10 minutes)
        </p>
        <p>
          In MAT 5610 you will be expected to read proofs like those in
          Bauldry and identify their logical structure. This section
          practices exactly that.
        </p>
      </introduction>

      <exercise xml:id="ex-mod1-br-10">
        <title>Theorem 2.2: Infimum Theorem</title>
        <statement>
          <p> Read the proof of Theorem 2.2 on page 47
            (the infimum theorem: if <m>B \subseteq U</m> is bounded
            below in a set with the LUB property, then <m>\inf B</m>
            exists and is in <m>U</m>).
            <ol marker="(a)">
              <li>
                State the hypothesis and conclusion of Theorem 2.2
                as an implication in the form <m>P \Rightarrow Q</m>.
              </li>
              <li>
                Bauldry's proof constructs a new set <m>L</m>. What
                is <m>L</m>, and what role does the LUB property play
                in the proof?
              </li>
              <li>
                Is this a direct proof, a proof by contrapositive, or
                a proof by contradiction? Justify your answer.
              </li>
            </ol>
          </p>
        </statement>
      </exercise>

      <exercise xml:id="ex-mod1-br-11">
        <title>Theorem 2.7: Open Sets Under Union</title>
        <statement>
          <p>
            Theorem 2.7 (p. 50) states four results about unions and
            intersections of open and closed sets. Consider part 1:
            <q>For any collection of open sets <m>\{O_\alpha\}</m>,
            the union <m>\bigcup_\alpha O_\alpha</m> is open.</q>
            <ol marker="(a)">
              <li>
                Write this statement using an explicit universal
                quantifier over points <m>x</m> in the union. (Hint:
                a set is open iff every point is an interior point,
                i.e., has a neighborhood contained in the set.)
              </li>
              <li>
                Trace through Bauldry's one-paragraph proof.
                Identify the moment the proof uses the definition
                of <q>open</q> and the moment it uses the definition
                of <q>union.</q>
              </li>
            </ol>
          </p>
        </statement>
      </exercise>

      <exercise xml:id="ex-mod1-br-12">
        <title>Completeness as an Iff</title>
        <statement>
          <p>
            Bauldry ends page 50 by noting that a metric space is
            <term>complete</term> <q>if and only if every Cauchy
            sequence converges.</q>
            <ol marker="(a)">
              <li>
                This is an iff definition. Write it with the biconditional
                arrow <m>\Leftrightarrow</m>.
              </li>
              <li>
                Based on your Module 1 work: to verify that a metric
                space <em>is</em> complete, which direction of the iff
                would you need to prove? To show it is <em>not</em>
                complete, what would you need to exhibit?
              </li>
            </ol>
          </p>
        </statement>
      </exercise>

      <introduction>
        <p>
          <alert>Part 4: Synthesis Reflection (pp. 45&#x2013;50)</alert>
          (Suggested time: 5 minutes)
        </p>
        <p>
          After completing the guided questions, take a few minutes for
          the following reflection. Write two to four sentences for each.
          These reflections are not graded but will directly prepare you
          for the Module 1 assessment.
        </p>
      </introduction>

      <exercise xml:id="ex-mod1-br-13">
        <title>Quantifiers Everywhere</title>
        <statement>
          <p> Look back at the six pages you just read.
            Count how many times the symbols <m>\forall</m> or
            <m>\exists</m> appear explicitly, or are implied by phrases
            like <q>for every,</q> <q>there exists,</q> <q>for any,</q>
            <q>whenever,</q> or <q>for some.</q> What does this count
            tell you about the role of quantifiers in graduate-level
            mathematics?
          </p>
        </statement>
      </exercise>

      <exercise xml:id="ex-mod1-br-14">
        <title>Flag an Unfamiliar Term</title>
        <statement>
          <p> Identify one definition or theorem from
            pp. 45&#x2013;50 that you could not fully parse after
            reading Module 1 alone. What specific term or concept
            is unfamiliar? (This is a preview of Modules 2 and 3;
            flagging it now will help you engage with those modules
            more deliberately.)
          </p>
        </statement>
      </exercise>

      <exercise xml:id="ex-mod1-br-15">
        <title>Contradiction and Existence Claims</title>
        <statement>
          <p>
            Consider the following sentence from Bauldry's proof of
            Theorem 2.3: <q>Suppose no such <m>n</m> exists.</q>
            <ol marker="(a)">
              <li>
                What proof strategy does this sentence signal?
              </li>
              <li>
                Write the formal negation of the conclusion of
                Theorem 2.3, i.e., the statement that Bauldry is
                temporarily assuming.
              </li>
              <li>
                Why is this strategy well-suited to an existence claim?
              </li>
            </ol>
          </p>
        </statement>
      </exercise>


  </section>
